// facet_gtk/controls — one GTK body per node kind.
//
// The shape is `facet_appkit/controls`': a `create_*` that builds the widget
// and an `apply_*` that writes the kind's own props, each gated on its dirty
// bit. The shared band (background, opacity, enabled, tooltip, radius) is NOT
// here — it is `paint::band`, applied around these by `views::configure`, so a
// kind cannot answer half of it.
//
// EVERY WRITE IS GATED. `dirty` on the create pass is `paint::all_bits()`, so
// the same code configures a new widget and updates a live one — one path, and
// the two cannot drift. That is the rule the AppKit backend states as "one
// code path with apply".
//
// ---- the CSS half is SEPARATE, and rebuilt whole ------------------------------
//
// GTK 4 has no per-declaration edit on a GtkCssProvider: writing a font drops
// the background that was already there. So a kind does NOT write CSS from its
// `apply_*`. It declares three things instead —
//
//   css_bits(kind)    which dirty bits change this kind's CSS
//   css_decls(kind)   the declarations for the widget's OWN node
//   css_rules(kind)   whole rules for the control's CSS SUBNODES
//
// — and `views::configure` composes them with the band's into ONE document,
// once, whenever any of those bits is dirty. That is what makes a
// background-only change keep the label's font, which the earlier
// write-as-you-go arrangement silently lost.
//
// This file is the growth surface. A kind is added by writing its two
// functions, its CSS trio, and naming it in `views::create_for_kind` /
// `apply_for_kind`; the coverage tool then counts the prop bits it references,
// and MANIFEST.md records anything GTK genuinely cannot answer.

